## Final report

## Reviewed
HEAD @ 8bc7eded2f4b0ea4f940d16a09c118fcc7f4b536 ("pricing: simpler split_bill"). One file changed: `tinyshop/pricing.py` (3 lines added, 6 removed). I got the diff with `git diff HEAD~1 HEAD`. The tree was clean before my runs.

## Findings (reproduced)
### F1: split_bill shares no longer add up to the total
Where: tinyshop/pricing.py:5-6
Command:
    python3 -c "from tinyshop.pricing import split_bill as s; print(s(100,3), sum(s(100,3))); print(s(200,3), sum(s(200,3))); print(s(10,4), sum(s(10,4)))"
Output (exit 0):
    [33, 33, 33] 99
    [67, 67, 67] 201
    [2, 2, 2, 2] 8
Expected: `sum(split_bill(t, n)) == t`. The docstring promises this ("whole-cent shares that add up to total_cents") and so does the commit message. With `round(total/people) * people`, a cent is lost or added whenever `total % people != 0`: 100 → 99 and 200 → 201. Python's round-half-to-even also turns 10/4 = 2.5 into 2, which loses 2 cents.
Fix: Put back `base, rem = divmod(total_cents, people); return [base + 1 if i < rem else base for i in range(people)]`. Also add a test that the shares sum to the total for an amount that doesn't split evenly, e.g. `split_bill(100, 3)`.

## Claims checked
| Claim | Source | Result | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| "Shares still add up to the total" | commit message | refuted | `s(100,3)` → `[33, 33, 33] 99`; `s(200,3)` → `[67, 67, 67] 201` |
| "One expression instead of divmod" (code is simpler) | commit message | confirmed (true as a description, but behaviour got worse) | the diff replaces divmod with `round(total_cents / people)` |

